<section class="bvrp-section" id="bitcoin-comparison">
  <h2>The Bitcoin <em>Comparison</em></h2>

  <p>Now the other side. This section is direct about bitcoin&rsquo;s weaknesses, and leans on the <a href="/the-power-law">Power Law</a> as the projection backbone &mdash; the same model that anchors the rest of this site.</p>

  <p>If bitcoin stays at today&rsquo;s multiple of its Power Law trend, with no reversion toward the trend assumed, the model implies growth of <strong data-pl-cagr="0,10">&mdash;</strong> a year over the next ten years and <strong data-pl-cagr="10,20">&mdash;</strong> a year over the ten after that (computed live from the site&rsquo;s Power Law coefficients). The rate falls every year as the curve flattens; <a href="/the-bitcoin-hurdle-rate">The Hurdle Rate</a> shows how fast. These are model outputs, not a record, and the model can be wrong. Bitcoin&rsquo;s liquidity profile is the inverse of real estate&rsquo;s: you can sell 1% of your position in seconds for an exchange spread and fee, on any day, from any device. A rental sells whole, over months, for about 6&ndash;9% in an orderly sale and more when it is forced (The Reality tab). The price you sell at is another matter: bitcoin&rsquo;s can move a long way in the months a house takes to close.</p>

  <p>What bitcoin doesn&rsquo;t carry: tenants, maintenance, local rent rules, and the hours they take. The asset is fungible, portable, divisible and censorship-resistant, and it works the same in Texas as in California as in Maine.</p>

  <p><strong>But this page has to name what bitcoin gives up to deliver this.</strong> Bitcoin&rsquo;s volatility is far higher than real estate&rsquo;s: housing&rsquo;s 9.86% standard deviation (the 16-country figure above) is a small fraction of bitcoin&rsquo;s, and bitcoin has been through repeated drawdowns of well over half its value. <em>Bitcoin trades volatility for absolute return.</em> Over long holding periods in the record so far, the growth differential has outweighed the volatility; nothing guarantees it will again. Over shorter windows, or for an investor who cannot sit through 50&ndash;80% drawdowns without selling, this trade does not work.</p>

  <p>The hassle-adjusted comparison, then: rental property&rsquo;s return comes with hours of attention and an operational load; bitcoin&rsquo;s comes with none of that and with deep drawdowns instead. Which trade suits you depends on your horizon, your temperament and your numbers, and the calculator in the last tab puts figures on both sides.</p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- SECTION 3.5: WHEN THIS DECISION IS BEST MADE -->
  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div id="when-decision-best" style="margin-top:2.4rem">
    <h2 style="font-size:1.8rem">When This Decision Is Best <em>Made</em></h2>

    <p>Entry timing matters. This page&rsquo;s thesis is structurally more compelling when bitcoin sits at or below long-term trend than when it sits in the upper band. Not a guarantee &mdash; the Power Law is a model, not a forecast &mdash; but the right guidance to surface before you choose a path.</p>

    <p>The Power Law channel describes bitcoin&rsquo;s long-term trajectory bounded by a trend line and a 0.42&times;&ndash;3&times; envelope. Bitcoin spends most of its time inside that channel, oscillating between phases of expensive (upper band) and cheap (around the floor). Entering near the floor means your forward CAGR is structurally larger; entering near the ceiling means it&rsquo;s structurally compressed. In the model, the long-run result holds from most entries; how quickly it arrives depends on where in the channel you bought.</p>

