<section class="bvrp-section" id="four-paths">
  <h2>Four Paths to <em>Switch</em></h2>

  <p>The reader sits somewhere on a conviction curve. This page doesn&rsquo;t recommend one path &mdash; it lays out four, with the tradeoffs, so the reader can self-select based on where they are.</p>

  <div class="bvrp-path">
    <h3>Path 1 &mdash; Outright Sell (Full Pivot)</h3>
    <div class="bvrp-path-sub">High conviction · No leverage · Highest tax friction</div>
    <p>Sell the rental property outright, pay the tax bill, redeploy net proceeds into bitcoin or a bitcoin-yield portfolio. The cleanest path, but the most expensive on day one.</p>
    <p>Tax math, worked example. A $500K property with a $200K adjusted basis (originally purchased for $300K, $100K depreciation taken) sells for $500K. After 6.6% in selling costs (the calculator&rsquo;s default: a 5.6% commission plus about 1% of other closing costs), net proceeds are $467K. The taxable gain is $267K: the $100K of depreciation is taxed at the ordinary rate capped at 25% (25% from the 32% bracket up, which this example assumes; 24% in the 24% bracket), so $25K, and the remaining $167K at 15% long-term capital gains ($25K). Total federal tax: $50K. Net cash to seller: <strong>$417K</strong>, a 17% all-in haircut at the federal level, before state tax and the 3.8% NIIT (which for a California investor in the top bracket, at 20% capital gains, brings total leakage to roughly 27%). With a mortgage on the property, the sale repays it first, as the calculator below does.</p>
    <p>Critically: <strong>1031 exchanges don&rsquo;t apply</strong>. Bitcoin is not &ldquo;like-kind&rdquo; property for 1031 purposes, and opportunity zones don&rsquo;t qualify. The tax-deferral pathways landlords normally rely on are closed for this destination.</p>
    <p><strong>Best for:</strong> high conviction; long holding horizon (5&ndash;10+ years to amortize the upfront tax); investors who are also tired of being landlords for non-financial reasons.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="bvrp-path">
    <h3>Path 2 &mdash; HELOC / Cash-Out Refi (Partial Levered)</h3>
    <div class="bvrp-path-sub">Lower conviction · Adds leverage · No tax event upfront</div>
    <p>Tap your home&rsquo;s equity via a HELOC (or cash-out refi) to buy bitcoin without selling the rental. No immediate tax event &mdash; loan proceeds are not income.</p>
    <p>Cash-out refinance is largely impractical in 2026. About half (49.9%) of outstanding US mortgages carry rates below 4% (<a href="https://www.fhfa.gov/data/national-mortgage-database-nmdb" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FHFA National Mortgage Database</a>, Q1 2026), and the 30-year fixed averaged 7.03% on September 24, 2026 (Freddie Mac PMMS), so replacing the entire balance for partial bitcoin exposure rarely pencils. <strong>A HELOC is the realistic tool.</strong> Lenders cap combined loan-to-value, commonly at 80&ndash;90% on a primary residence, and HELOC rates are variable, tied to the Prime Rate (7.00% on September 21, 2026). Bankrate&rsquo;s national average HELOC rate was 7.28% on September 23, 2026.</p>
    <p><strong>Tax treatment caveat:</strong> Under the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act, HELOC interest is only deductible as mortgage interest if the proceeds are used to &ldquo;buy, build, or improve&rdquo; the home itself. Proceeds used to buy bitcoin are not deductible. They <em>could</em> be deducted as investment-interest expense if you have offsetting investment income &mdash; but spot bitcoin generates no investment income (it pays no dividends or interest), making this deduction largely unavailable. Plan accordingly.</p>
    <p><strong>The reframe.</strong> A HELOC-funded bitcoin position is structurally a <em>leveraged bitcoin position collateralized by your home</em>. At 80% combined LTV, your home&rsquo;s untouched 20% equity is more than your $100K bitcoin position, so a 100% bitcoin wipeout cannot cause negative equity from bitcoin alone. The real risk is <strong>debt-service insolvency</strong> through a multi-year drawdown &mdash; paying about $607 a month in interest on a $100K draw (at 7.28%) out of personal income while your bitcoin position sits down 70&ndash;80%. The 2022 cycle is the canonical exhibit: bitcoin dropped 77% precisely as variable HELOC rates were climbing in response to Fed tightening. A double shock, and the worst-case scenario for this strategy.</p>
    <p><strong>Best for:</strong> investors who want partial exposure, can&rsquo;t or won&rsquo;t sell the property, have stable non-rental income sufficient to service the debt through a multi-year drawdown, and understand they are taking a leveraged position.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="bvrp-path">
    <h3>Path 3 &mdash; Partial Portfolio Sale</h3>
    <div class="bvrp-path-sub">For multi-property landlords · Path 1 economics on one property</div>
    <p>Sell one rental property, take the tax hit on that one only, redeploy into bitcoin. Keep the rest of the portfolio.</p>
    <p>Per-property tax math is identical to Path 1. The advantage is avoiding the all-or-nothing decision: you preserve some rental cash flow on the remaining properties while gaining bitcoin exposure on the proceeds of the sold one. This is probably the most palatable path for landlords with 2&ndash;5 properties, especially if one of those properties is the most operationally annoying (worst tenants, oldest building, worst regulatory jurisdiction).</p>
    <p><strong>Best for:</strong> moderate conviction; multi-property portfolio; investors who don&rsquo;t want to lose all rental cash flow but want meaningful bitcoin exposure. Mental load is partially retained because you still manage the remaining properties.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="bvrp-path">
    <h3>Path 4 &mdash; Sell Rental, Buy Bitcoin Yield Portfolio</h3>
    <div class="bvrp-path-sub">Full pivot, cash flow preserved · The headline option for income-dependent investors</div>
    <p>Combine Path 1&rsquo;s outright sale with the yield-instrument substitution from the Bitcoin Case tab. Take the tax hit; redeploy net proceeds into a portfolio of STRC, SATA, optional CeFi, and a small spot bitcoin allocation. The yield this portfolio generates is materially higher than the net rental cash flow you gave up &mdash; even after eating the upfront tax leakage.</p>
    <p>For an income-dependent investor who can&rsquo;t go without monthly cash flow, this is the path that keeps an income stream closest to the rental&rsquo;s. You give up local-tenant-and-property risk and take on issuer-credit risk &mdash; a different risk concentration, but at higher yield, with comparable tax treatment, and with no operational load.</p>
    <p><strong>Best for:</strong> income-dependent investors; those who want to fully exit landlording without giving up the cash flow stream that made rental property attractive in the first place.</p>
  </div>
</section>

