      <div class="calc-slider-group calc-path-specific" data-for-path="2">
        <div class="calc-slider-group-label">HELOC draw</div>
        <div class="calc-slider-row">
          <span class="calc-slider-name">Max combined LTV
            <span class="help-tip" tabindex="0">?<span class="tip-content">
              Combined Loan-to-Value: how much of your home&rsquo;s appraised value can be borrowed across all liens (mortgage + HELOC). The HELOC draw is this LTV cap minus your existing mortgage balance. Typical lender ceilings: 80&ndash;90% for primary residences.
            </span></span>
          </span>
          <span class="calc-slider-val" id="val-heloc-ltv">80%</span>
        </div>
        <input type="range" id="calc-heloc-ltv" min="50" max="90" step="5" value="80">
      </div>

      <!-- Path 3-specific active. Two sliders (Total + Retained) with the
           sold count derived. Cleaner mental model than the prior "how many
           to sell" formulation, which the prior version of this section
           allowed to exceed the actual total. -->
      <div class="calc-slider-group calc-path-specific" data-for-path="3">
        <div class="calc-slider-group-label">Partial sale composition</div>

        <div class="calc-slider-row">
          <span class="calc-slider-name">Total # of properties</span>
          <span class="calc-slider-val" id="val-num-properties">3 properties</span>
        </div>
        <input type="range" id="calc-num-properties" min="2" max="10" step="1" value="3">

        <div class="calc-slider-row">
          <span class="calc-slider-name">Properties retained as rentals</span>
          <span class="calc-slider-val" id="val-properties-retained">2 retained</span>
        </div>
        <input type="range" id="calc-properties-retained" min="1" max="2" step="1" value="2">

        <div class="calc-path3-derived" id="calc-path3-derived">
          &rarr; <strong id="calc-path3-sold-count">1 property</strong> sold &mdash; proceeds redeploy into the bitcoin yield portfolio.
        </div>
        <div class="calc-path3-note">
          Each property assumed to be worth the &ldquo;Property value&rdquo; slider amount above. &ldquo;Keep rental&rdquo; in this path means keeping <em>all</em> properties (apples-to-apples vs. selling some).
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Path 3+4 portfolio composition (active) -->
      <div class="calc-slider-group calc-path-specific" data-for-path="3,4">
        <div class="calc-slider-group-label">Bitcoin yield portfolio</div>

        <div class="calc-slider-row">
          <span class="calc-slider-name">STRC <span class="calc-slider-hint">(Strategy, <span data-yr="strc">12%</span>, ROC)</span>
            <span class="help-tip" tabindex="0">?<span class="tip-content">
              Strategy&rsquo;s variable-rate Series&nbsp;A perpetual preferred stock. Paying 12.00% annualized, semi-monthly (September 2026 8-K). Strategy reported 100% of its 2025 preferred distributions as return of capital and expects that treatment to continue. Return of capital is tax-deferred, not tax-free: it lowers your cost basis instead of being taxed as income now, and raises the gain when you sell.
            </span></span>
          </span>
          <span class="calc-slider-val" id="val-port-strc">45%</span>
        </div>
        <input type="range" id="calc-port-strc" min="0" max="100" step="5" value="45">

        <div class="calc-slider-row">
          <span class="calc-slider-name">SATA <span class="calc-slider-hint">(Strive, <span data-yr="sata">13%</span>, ROC)</span>
            <span class="help-tip" tabindex="0">?<span class="tip-content">
              Strive&rsquo;s variable-rate Series&nbsp;A perpetual preferred stock. Pays 13.00% annualized, every business day since June 16, 2026, backed by Strive&rsquo;s 26,355 BTC treasury (September 18, 2026) and a dividend reserve. For 2026, Strive expects 100% of distributions to be return of capital, to the extent of your basis (its Forms 8937); the final treatment is set after year-end. Tax-deferred, not tax-free.
            </span></span>
          </span>
          <span class="calc-slider-val" id="val-port-sata">30%</span>
        </div>
        <input type="range" id="calc-port-sata" min="0" max="100" step="5" value="30">

        <div class="calc-slider-row">
          <span class="calc-slider-name">Stablecoin lending <span class="calc-slider-hint">(<span data-yr="lending">4%</span>, ordinary)</span>
            <span class="help-tip" tabindex="0">?<span class="tip-content">
              Interest on dollar stablecoins (USDC, USDT) lent through a platform or a protocol. Not bitcoin, and no return-of-capital shield: it is taxed as ordinary income, so the after-tax yield shrinks at higher brackets. The default, <span data-yr="lending">4%</span>, is the middle of the rates that can be checked from outside, listed under the calculator (<span data-yr="asof">September 2026</span>); it is about what a 3-month Treasury bill pays, so the extra risks (platform failure, smart-contract failure, loss of the dollar peg) are not currently being paid for. Ledn&rsquo;s Growth Account pays more but is not available to US residents.
            </span></span>
          </span>
          <span class="calc-slider-val" id="val-port-lend">10%</span>
        </div>
        <input type="range" id="calc-port-lend" min="0" max="100" step="5" value="10">

        <div class="calc-slider-row">
          <span class="calc-slider-name">Spot BTC <span class="calc-slider-hint">(no distributions)</span>
            <span class="help-tip" tabindex="0">?<span class="tip-content">
              Bitcoin held outright (self-custody or via spot ETF). No cash distributions; pure asset appreciation. The portfolio&rsquo;s growth engine &mdash; the slice that captures bitcoin&rsquo;s structural CAGR while the yield instruments provide income.
            </span></span>
          </span>
          <span class="calc-slider-val" id="val-port-spot">15%</span>
        </div>
        <input type="range" id="calc-port-spot" min="0" max="100" step="5" value="15">

        <div class="calc-slider-row calc-port-total-row">
          <span class="calc-slider-name">Total allocation</span>
          <span class="calc-slider-val" id="calc-port-total" style="color:var(--green)">100%</span>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>

    <!-- Display frame (PR 4f; design §7, rulings M1, P5): the same toggle, help
         tip and frame line as Bitcoin vs. Real Estate, from
         components/real-estate-baseline.njk and shared/real-estate-baseline.js.
         Everything is computed in nominal dollars; Real divides each year's
         value by that year's factor at the deflator set in Baseline
         assumptions. The frame line says which view is showing and what Real
         means next to Nominal (JM, 2026-09-29); the binder rewrites it from
         the live deflator, so the words here are first paint only. -->
    {% import 'components/real-estate-baseline.njk' as reb %}
    <div class="calc-frame-row">
      <span class="calc-frame-label">Display values in
        {{ reb.frameTip('bvrp') }}
      </span>
      <div class="calc-frame-toggle" role="group" aria-label="Display values in">
        <button type="button" class="calc-frame-btn active" data-mode="real" aria-pressed="true">Real (today&rsquo;s $)</button>
        <button type="button" class="calc-frame-btn" data-mode="nominal" aria-pressed="false">Nominal (future $)</button>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="calc-deflator-line"><span id="rpDeflatorLine">Showing <strong>Real</strong> values: today&rsquo;s dollars, deflated at 6.5% a year (M2 growth). At that rate, $100,000 ten years from now buys what $53,273 buys today, so Real shows $53,273 where Nominal shows $100,000.</span> <a href="#rpBaseline" class="calc-deflator-change" id="rpDeflatorChange">Change it</a></p>

    <!-- Bitcoin scenario picker (chips above chart). One set on both pages
         (rulings M3, PR 4c): Floor · Stay at today's multiple (default) ·
         Trend · Upper, each moving bitcoin's multiple of the Power Law trend
         in a straight line from today's to a target at the horizon end. -->
    <div class="calc-cagr-picker">
      <div class="calc-cagr-picker-row">
        <div class="calc-cagr-picker-label">
          Bitcoin scenario, based on the Power Law
          <span class="help-tip" tabindex="0">?<span class="tip-content">
            All four scenarios are anchored to the Power Law model and recalibrate as bitcoin&rsquo;s multiple of the trend moves. Each moves that multiple in a straight line from today&rsquo;s to a target at the end of the holding period. The selected chip drives the headline number and the comparison table. Floor is always drawn faintly on the chart; the legend shows or hides any line. All four scenarios assume the Power Law trend itself continues.
          </span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="calc-cagr-picker-current">
          BTC today: <strong id="calc-current-multiple">&mdash;</strong>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="calc-cagr-chips" id="calc-cagr-chips">
        <button class="calc-cagr-chip" data-scenario="floor">
          <span class="calc-cagr-chip-name">Floor
            <span class="help-tip calc-chip-help" tabindex="0" data-scenario-tip="floor">?<span class="tip-content"></span></span>
          </span>
          <span class="calc-cagr-chip-rate">&mdash;</span>
          <span class="calc-cagr-chip-sub">ends at 0.42&times; trend</span>
        </button>
        <button class="calc-cagr-chip active" data-scenario="stay">
          <span class="calc-cagr-chip-name">Stay at today&rsquo;s <span class="calc-cagr-chip-nowrap">multiple
            <span class="help-tip calc-chip-help" tabindex="0" data-scenario-tip="stay">?<span class="tip-content"></span></span></span>
          </span>
          <span class="calc-cagr-chip-rate">&mdash;</span>
          <span class="calc-cagr-chip-sub">no reversion either way</span>
        </button>
        <button class="calc-cagr-chip" data-scenario="trend">
          <span class="calc-cagr-chip-name">Trend
            <span class="help-tip calc-chip-help" tabindex="0" data-scenario-tip="trend">?<span class="tip-content"></span></span>
          </span>
          <span class="calc-cagr-chip-rate">&mdash;</span>
          <span class="calc-cagr-chip-sub">ends at 1&times; trend</span>
        </button>
        <button class="calc-cagr-chip" data-scenario="upper">
          <span class="calc-cagr-chip-name">Upper
            <span class="help-tip calc-chip-help" tabindex="0" data-scenario-tip="upper">?<span class="tip-content"></span></span>
          </span>
          <span class="calc-cagr-chip-rate">&mdash;</span>
          <span class="calc-cagr-chip-sub">ends at 2.5&times; trend</span>
        </button>
      </div>
      <p class="calc-chips-frame" id="calc-chips-frame" hidden>Growth rates are nominal; the differences are in today&rsquo;s dollars.</p>
      <a class="calc-cagr-pl-link" href="/the-power-law">What is the Power Law? &rarr;</a>
    </div>

    <!-- Chart — the centerpiece. Wealth over time, four bitcoin lines + rental (Floor faint unless selected). -->
    <div class="calc-chart-container">
      <div class="calc-chart-header">
        <div class="calc-chart-title">Wealth over time, your scenario <span class="calc-chart-frame" id="calc-chart-frame">(real, today&rsquo;s $)</span></div>
        <div class="calc-chart-zoom calc-chart-basis" role="group" aria-label="What the lines value">
          <button class="calc-chart-basis-btn active" data-basis="ifsold" aria-pressed="true">If sold, after tax</button>
          <button class="calc-chart-basis-btn" data-basis="held" aria-pressed="false">Held</button>
        </div>
        <div class="calc-chart-zoom" role="tablist" aria-label="Chart zoom range">
          <button class="calc-chart-zoom-btn active" data-zoom="full" role="tab" aria-selected="true">Full window</button>
          <button class="calc-chart-zoom-btn" data-zoom="first3" role="tab" aria-selected="false">First 3 years</button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="calc-chart-wrap" data-chart-copy data-chart-heading=".calc-chart-title" data-chart-assump="#calc-headline" data-chart-title="Bitcoin vs. rental property — wealth over time">
        <canvas id="calc-chart"></canvas>
      </div>
      <div class="calc-chart-caveat">
        <p><strong>What the lines value.</strong> <em>If sold, after tax</em> (the default) sells both sides in each year: the rental after selling costs, the mortgage and its exit tax (recapture and the gain), bitcoin after its sale cost and the tax on its gain, STRC and SATA at par with the gain their return of capital left in the lower basis. <em>Held</em> is before any sale: the rental&rsquo;s market value less the mortgage, bitcoin at its value. The bold light line is the <strong>difference</strong>, the selected path minus keeping the rental: above zero, the path is ahead. Bitcoin paths pay the rental&rsquo;s sale tax and repay its mortgage at Y0, so they start lower; the <em>First 3 years</em> view shows that early dip.</p>
        <p><strong>Tax asymmetry on realization.</strong> If both positions are eventually liquidated at year N, the exit-tax profiles differ: a rental sale incurs depreciation recapture (the calculator taxes accumulated depreciation at your bracket&rsquo;s rate, capped at 25%) and selling costs (6.6% by default, within The Reality tab&rsquo;s 6&ndash;9% range for an orderly sale: a 5.6% agent commission plus about 1% of other seller closing costs; set in Baseline assumptions) <em>in addition</em> to LTCG + state cap gains. A bitcoin sale faces only LTCG + state cap gains &mdash; a structurally lighter exit-tax profile. The chart (by default), the headline and the table&rsquo;s <em>If sold</em> rows sell both sides and take the tax off; the chart&rsquo;s <em>Held</em> view shows values before any sale.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="calc-chart-legend" id="calc-chart-legend"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- Headline verdict -->
    <div id="calc-headline" class="calc-headline">&hellip;</div>

    <!-- Comparison table -->
    <div class="calc-comparison">
      <table class="bvrp-table calc-comparison-table">
        <thead id="calc-comparison-headers"></thead>
        <tbody id="calc-comparison-body"></tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="calc-comparison-frame" id="calc-comparison-frame"></p>
    </div>

    <!-- Path-specific detail card -->
    <div id="calc-path-detail" class="calc-path-detail"></div>

    <!-- The sensitivity grid (PR 7; rulings R9, P1). -->
    <div id="calc-grid"></div>

    <!-- Show the calculation (PR 6a; rulings P2): the rental and the path, year by year. -->
    <div id="calc-ledger"></div>

    <!-- Specific-to-you callout -->
    <div id="calc-specific-callout" class="calc-specific-callout"></div>

    <!-- PR 8 (rulings P3): the pair's shared inputs, carried to the tenant's
         page by shared/real-estate-carry.js. Its projection is the forward
         calculator that matches this one. -->
    <p class="calc-other-side"><a href="/bitcoin-vs-real-estate#projection" data-re-carry="/bitcoin-vs-real-estate" data-re-carry-hash="projection">Run this on the other side &rarr;</a> Bitcoin vs. Real Estate, the tenant&rsquo;s version of the decision, opens its projection with your holding period as the horizon, and your bitcoin scenario, home appreciation, sale costs, Real or Nominal view, and tax regime, bracket and state.</p>

    <!-- Baseline assumptions (collapsible — set once, ignore) — moved to the very bottom per TBR pattern.
         The first two groups are the pair's shared, sitewide part (PR 4f; design §7, rulings M1, P5):
         their words and presets come from components/real-estate-baseline.njk and
         shared/real-estate-baseline.js binds them, as on Bitcoin vs. Real Estate
         (the macros are imported above the display frame). -->
    <details class="calc-baseline" id="rpBaseline">
      <summary>
        <span class="calc-baseline-summary-label">Baseline assumptions</span>
        <span class="calc-baseline-summary-hint" id="rpBaselineHint">home prices 4.68% a year nominal (since 2000) &middot; Real view deflated at 6.5% (M2 growth)</span>
      </summary>

      <div class="calc-baseline-grid">

        <!-- Home appreciation (PR 4a, rulings M1): the pair's shared NOMINAL
             input, lcs.homeApprNominal; was a hardcoded 3.0%. -->
        <div class="calc-slider-group">
          <div class="calc-slider-group-label">Home appreciation</div>
          <div class="calc-slider-row">
            <span class="calc-slider-name">Rate <span class="calc-slider-hint">(nominal / yr)</span> {{ reb.apprTip('bvrp') }}</span>
            <input type="text" id="rpHomeAppreciation" class="calc-appr-input" value="4.68%" inputmode="decimal" aria-label="Home appreciation, nominal percent a year">
          </div>
          {{ reb.apprPresets('rp', 'calc-appr-presets', 'calc-appr-preset', '') }}
          <p class="calc-appr-notice" id="rpApprNotice" hidden></p>
        </div>

        <!-- The deflator for the Real view (PR 4f, P5): the sitewide
             lcs.inflation. It drives only the Real view. -->
        <div class="calc-slider-group">
          <div class="calc-slider-group-label">Deflator for the Real view</div>
          <div class="calc-slider-row">
            <span class="calc-slider-name">Rate <span class="calc-slider-hint">(% / yr)</span> {{ reb.inflTip('bvrp') }}</span>
            <input type="text" id="rpInflation" class="calc-appr-input" value="6.5%" inputmode="decimal" aria-label="Deflator for the Real view, percent a year">
          </div>
          {{ reb.inflPresets('rp', 'calc-appr-presets', 'calc-appr-preset', '') }}
        </div>

        <div class="calc-slider-group">
          <div class="calc-slider-group-label">Tax profile</div>

          <div class="calc-slider-row">
            <span class="calc-slider-name">Tax regime {{ reb.regimeTip('bvrp') }}</span>
            <select id="calc-tax-regime" class="calc-select">
              {{ reb.regimeOptions('bvrp') }}
            </select>
          </div>

          <div class="calc-slider-row">
            <span class="calc-slider-name">State
              <span class="help-tip" tabindex="0">?<span class="tip-content">
                The state rate applied to rental income, lending interest and the gain on a sale. Default is a typical ~5% rate &mdash; select your actual state for an accurate calculation. Rates vary widely (0% in TX/FL/NV/WA/TN to 13.3% in CA).
              </span></span>
            </span>
            <select id="calc-state" class="calc-select">
              {{ reb.stateOptions() }}
            </select>
          </div>

          <div class="calc-slider-row">
            <span class="calc-slider-name">Federal bracket {{ reb.bracketTip() }}</span>
            <select id="calc-bracket" class="calc-select">
              {{ reb.bracketOptions() }}
            </select>
          </div>
          {{ reb.notTaxAdvice('calc-tax-note') }}
        </div>

        <div class="calc-slider-group">
          <div class="calc-slider-group-label">Property facts</div>

          <div class="calc-slider-row">
            <span class="calc-slider-name">Adjusted basis
              <span class="help-tip" tabindex="0">?<span class="tip-content">
                Your property&rsquo;s tax basis: original purchase price plus capital improvements, minus depreciation already taken. Drives the taxable gain on sale. From this and the years held, the calculator works out the original building basis (80% of the cost basis) that depreciation runs on. Default 60% of current value approximates 10 years of holding with steady appreciation.
              </span></span>
            </span>
            <span class="calc-slider-val" id="val-adjusted-basis">60%</span>
          </div>
          <input type="range" id="calc-adjusted-basis" min="10" max="100" step="5" value="60">

          <div class="calc-slider-row">
            <span class="calc-slider-name">Years already held
              <span class="help-tip" tabindex="0">?<span class="tip-content">
                How long you&rsquo;ve owned the property. Determines accumulated straight-line depreciation (building basis &divide; 27.5 yrs &times; years held), which becomes the depreciation-recapture tax bill at sale.
              </span></span>
            </span>
            <span class="calc-slider-val" id="val-years-held">10 yrs</span>
          </div>
          <input type="range" id="calc-years-held" min="0" max="27" step="1" value="10">
        </div>

        <!-- PR 4d (rulings M8): the existing mortgage applies on every path.
             Kept rentals carry it (payments off cash flow, interest deductible,
             the balance off equity); sales repay it before redeploying. Was a
             Path 2-only input. Defaults: RealEstateModel.RENTAL_DEFAULTS. -->
        <div class="calc-slider-group">
          <div class="calc-slider-group-label">Existing mortgage <span class="calc-slider-hint calc-perprop-hint" style="display:none">(per property)</span></div>

          <div class="calc-slider-row">
            <span class="calc-slider-name">Balance
              <span class="help-tip" tabindex="0">?<span class="tip-content">
                What you still owe on the property. Default $200,000, 40% of the $500,000 default value; the average outstanding US mortgage is about 45% of its home&rsquo;s value (FHFA National Mortgage Database, Q1 2026). Keeping the rental carries it: the payments come off cash flow, the interest is deductible against rental income, and the balance comes off the property&rsquo;s value. Every sale path repays it before anything is redeployed. On Path 2 it also sets the HELOC&rsquo;s room. Set it to $0 for a property owned outright.
              </span></span>
            </span>
            <span class="calc-slider-val" id="val-existing-mortgage">$200,000</span>
          </div>
          <input type="range" id="calc-existing-mortgage" min="0" max="2000000" step="10000" value="200000">

          <div class="calc-slider-row">
            <span class="calc-slider-name">Rate
              <span class="help-tip" tabindex="0">?<span class="tip-content">
                The mortgage&rsquo;s interest rate. Default 4.4%, the average rate on outstanding US mortgages (FHFA National Mortgage Database, Q1 2026). Loans on rental properties usually cost more than loans on homes; enter yours.
              </span></span>
            </span>
            <span class="calc-slider-val" id="val-mortgage-rate">4.4%</span>
          </div>
          <input type="range" id="calc-mortgage-rate" min="0" max="12" step="0.1" value="4.4">

          <div class="calc-slider-row">
            <span class="calc-slider-name">Years left
              <span class="help-tip" tabindex="0">?<span class="tip-content">
                Years until the mortgage is paid off. Default 20: a 30-year loan taken when the property was bought, ten years ago (the &ldquo;years already held&rdquo; default). If it ends within the holding period, the payments stop then.
              </span></span>
            </span>
            <span class="calc-slider-val" id="val-mortgage-years">20 yrs</span>
          </div>
          <input type="range" id="calc-mortgage-years" min="1" max="30" step="1" value="20">
        </div>

        <!-- PR 4b (rulings M6): one selling-cost default on both pages of the
             pair (was a hardcoded 8%), and a cost on each bitcoin purchase. -->
        <div class="calc-slider-group">
          <div class="calc-slider-group-label">Selling and trading</div>

          <div class="calc-slider-row">
            <span class="calc-slider-name">Selling costs <span class="calc-slider-hint">(% of the sale price)</span>
              <span class="help-tip" tabindex="0">?<span class="tip-content">
                Agent commission plus other seller closing costs on any property sale. Default 6.6%: a 5.6% commission (the midpoint of Clever Real Estate&rsquo;s two 2026 agent surveys, 5.70% and 5.46%) plus about 1% of other seller closing costs, an estimate: there is no national figure, and state transfer taxes dominate it. The same default as Bitcoin vs. Real Estate.
              </span></span>
            </span>
            <span class="calc-slider-val" id="val-sell-cost">6.6%</span>
          </div>
          <input type="range" id="calc-sell-cost" min="0" max="10" step="0.1" value="6.6">

          <div class="calc-slider-row">
            <span class="calc-slider-name">Bitcoin costs <span class="calc-slider-hint">(% per purchase)</span>
              <span class="help-tip" tabindex="0">?<span class="tip-content">
                Spread and fees on each bitcoin purchase, so the rental isn&rsquo;t the only side paying to trade. Default 0.5%, an estimate. Applied when the sale proceeds (or a HELOC draw) buy bitcoin; the chart and table value bitcoin before any sale, as they value the rental.
              </span></span>
            </span>
            <span class="calc-slider-val" id="val-btc-tx">0.5%</span>
          </div>
          <input type="range" id="calc-btc-tx" min="0" max="2" step="0.05" value="0.5">
        </div>

        <div class="calc-slider-group calc-path-specific" data-for-path="2">
          <div class="calc-slider-group-label">Path 2 &mdash; HELOC terms</div>

          <div class="calc-slider-row">
            <span class="calc-slider-name">HELOC rate <span class="calc-slider-hint">(lender quote)</span></span>
            <span class="calc-slider-val" id="val-heloc-rate">9.5%</span>
          </div>
          <input type="range" id="calc-heloc-rate" min="5" max="15" step="0.25" value="9.5">
        </div>

      </div>
    </details>

  </div>

  <!-- PR 4d (rulings §7, item 12): what the verifiable stablecoin-lending
       instruments pay this month. Rows come from RealEstateModel.YIELD_RATES,
       the one dated object the monthly refresh edits. -->
  <div class="calc-rates-note" id="calc-rates-note">
    <div class="calc-rates-title">Stablecoin lending: what the verifiable rates are (<span data-yr="asof">September 2026</span>)</div>
    <p>The calculator&rsquo;s lending slice earns <span data-yr="lending">4%</span>, the middle of the rates below that anyone can check. Base rates only: token rewards some pools add are promotional, not lending income, and are left out.</p>
    <div class="bvrp-table-wrap">
      <table class="bvrp-table calc-rates-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Rate</th><th>Open to US residents</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody id="calc-rates-rows"></tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p><strong>At current rates, stablecoin lending pays about what a Treasury bill pays.</strong> The extra risks &ndash; platform failure (Celsius, BlockFi and Voyager in 2022), smart-contract failure and loss of the dollar peg &ndash; are not currently being paid for. US platforms&rsquo; own rates can&rsquo;t be checked from outside, so they aren&rsquo;t listed: Coinbase shows its USDC rate only in the account, to paid Coinbase One members, and Nexo&rsquo;s &ldquo;up to&rdquo; rate depends on holding its own token.</p>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top:1.6rem;font-size:0.85rem;color:var(--ink-dim);font-style:italic">
    <strong>Model simplifications:</strong> Bitcoin scenarios are anchored to the site&rsquo;s Power Law model and dynamically recalibrate to bitcoin&rsquo;s current multiple-of-trend. Each scenario moves bitcoin&rsquo;s multiple of the trend in a straight line from today&rsquo;s to its target over the holding period, a simplification: in the record, reversion has been irregular in timing. The default, Stay at today&rsquo;s multiple, assumes no reversion. Return of capital is tax-deferred, not tax-free: the calculator lowers each instrument&rsquo;s basis by its distributions, and once the basis is used up (after about eight years at current STRC/SATA rates) taxes later distributions as long-term gains in the year paid. The gain left in the lower basis falls due at a sale, which these held values don&rsquo;t yet show. Cash received along the way (the kept rental&rsquo;s cash flow, the yield portfolio&rsquo;s distributions) is added up at face value and earns nothing while held, on every path; in the Real view it is deflated with the rest of each year&rsquo;s total, so it loses purchasing power at the deflator&rsquo;s rate. State taxes simplified to a single rate per state, applied to rental income, lending interest and gains. HELOC modeled as interest-only with balloon repayment.
  </p>
</section>

